I'm going to just close this for a minute, and I'm going to show you in side by

00:18:46.998 --> 00:18:51.543
side. So this view is sort of showing you the left is what it was and the right is

00:18:51.558 --> 00:18:54.738
what it became. And so there's a way to see.

00:18:54.948 --> 00:18:59.478
You can go back and see what things have changed, whether you forgot or whether

00:18:59.493 --> 00:19:03.048
someone else made a change. You can then go back and see exactly what changed.

00:19:03.108 --> 00:19:07.473
So this is the big power of Git and a lot of its users to sort of keep track of

00:19:07.518 --> 00:19:11.943
changes in an organized way. So those are called commits.

00:19:12.048 --> 00:19:14.149
They're all sequential,

00:19:15.528 --> 00:19:19.248
so always the new one is your most recent. That's what's sort of published.

00:19:19.308 --> 00:19:23.328
That's what's the latest version, and that's when you edit files, you're editing

00:19:23.358 --> 00:19:24.228
the latest version.

00:19:27.318 --> 00:19:31.518
You can go back to previous files, so it's called reverting.

00:19:32.089 --> 00:19:37.399
That's a little rabbit hole. For small things, you can sometimes open the commit

00:19:37.428 --> 00:19:39.438
and just copy the line that you accidentally deleted.

00:19:40.008 --> 00:19:44.058
If you made a lot of changes that you want to undo, there's sort of a revert

00:19:44.118 --> 00:19:47.958
function, and yeah, that's something you can do your research to do.

00:19:52.218 --> 00:19:52.638
So now,

00:19:54.138 --> 00:19:55.758
yeah, so this is, as I said, is GitLab.

00:19:56.089 --> 00:19:57.483
And to answer the question,

00:19:59.508 --> 00:20:04.758
GitLab is a wraparound Git, which means we can also have a copy of this Git

00:20:05.659 --> 00:20:09.753
project on our computer. And the great thing about copying this to our-- and it's

00:20:09.768 --> 00:20:13.458
not really a copy. Yeah, it's a copy to our computer, is that we can then just work

00:20:13.518 --> 00:20:18.018
on our computer without having the internet or without using the browser.

00:20:18.888 --> 00:20:21.348
So it can be faster depending on your computer.

00:20:22.083 --> 00:20:27.048
And then you get to decide every time you want to make those commits and push them.

00:20:27.768 --> 00:20:32.943
Whereas when you're working in a browser, because your window tab can close or

00:20:32.988 --> 00:20:36.138
crash or your computer restarts, you're sort of saving every time you make a

00:20:36.198 --> 00:20:40.848
change, a bit like Google Sheets. When you're working on a computer, you can do a

00:20:40.909 --> 00:20:45.453
full day of edits, as if you're editing some notes or files on your computer, and

00:20:45.498 --> 00:20:49.683
then you say, "Okay, I'm ready. I like how it looks." And then we call it push.

00:20:49.758 --> 00:20:54.513
You push it back up to the server, and then it sort of syncs with this project.

00:20:55.353 --> 00:20:59.388
And you can even work both with the browser and the local, but then you've got to

00:21:00.468 --> 00:21:02.928
remember which one's ahead and you've got to keep them in sync.

00:21:03.318 --> 00:21:04.818
So Git's designed for that. So yes,

00:21:07.008 --> 00:21:12.558
I'll get into it, but I do suggest trying local and we'll-- Yeah.

00:21:13.938 --> 00:21:17.238
Okay. So Zinkl is

00:21:20.028 --> 00:21:24.618
how the project is organized. So we saw in the repo we had a

00:21:25.668 --> 00:21:30.858
TOML file. This is basically how we sort of configure your

00:21:31.097 --> 00:21:35.839
personal website. So you can say stuff like the name of the website, the URL it's

00:21:35.928 --> 00:21:41.118
under, the colors, the fonts. You can add extra features.

00:21:41.208 --> 00:21:43.053
I don't want a table of contents. You

00:21:44.778 --> 00:21:48.168
can add extra functionality that's off by default, a lightbox.

00:21:48.198 --> 00:21:54.018
And so all those sort of general settings for the whole website happen in this

00:21:54.078 --> 00:21:54.738
TOML file.

00:21:56.808 --> 00:21:59.568
Then you have the docs folder, and this is where all the content goes.

00:21:59.659 --> 00:22:04.758
So this is where all your images, your files, this is where all the notes that you

00:22:04.774 --> 00:22:07.159
write in Markdown all go into the docs folder.

00:22:09.318 --> 00:22:09.678
And

00:22:10.909 --> 00:22:14.628
each of these files is ending in an MD, so as in the next line here.

00:22:14.718 --> 00:22:19.308
So as an example, index.md is one of the files that we have Markdown on.

00:22:19.909 --> 00:22:22.848
Index is a special word. It means home page.

00:22:23.839 --> 00:22:29.808
So index is the only one where if you write index in the URL, so for example,

00:22:29.839 --> 00:22:34.818
if I'm looking at my website, I only have a forward slash. You see I have index.

00:22:36.498 --> 00:22:37.308
And then

00:22:38.808 --> 00:22:44.238
your browser is tricking you because actually what's behind there is index.html.

00:22:44.253 --> 00:22:45.409
This is the actual file.

00:22:47.149 --> 00:22:52.938
When I have another file, for example, if I open my editor, you'll see there's a

00:22:52.998 --> 00:22:58.173
folder called Assignments, and then there's a file called Markdown Week 1.

00:23:00.409 --> 00:23:05.868
If I go to Assignments and then Week 1, I will see in my URL also

00:23:05.958 --> 00:23:09.649
Assignments and Week 1. But I don't have the HTML.

00:23:12.108 --> 00:23:16.548
So the file name that you have in the Markdown, it becomes the URL.

00:23:16.758 --> 00:23:20.478
So that's very important to map in your head, and you'll see that as you work

00:23:20.508 --> 00:23:21.078
through the weeks.

00:23:23.958 --> 00:23:29.598
And then we have this .gitlab-ci file, which is a special file for the project.

00:23:29.659 --> 00:23:35.583
This is basically telling GitLab, "Hey, this project is using Zinkl, and

00:23:35.628 --> 00:23:40.008
this is how you build the website. This is the command you use and how to upload it

00:23:40.068 --> 00:23:44.013
to our platform." So it's very specific to your project, and you don't really need

00:23:44.058 --> 00:23:49.608
to change anything there unless something goes wrong or you decide to do something

00:23:49.638 --> 00:23:52.998
completely different, use another tool, which is your prerogative.

00:23:53.058 --> 00:23:55.818
You can do whatever you want. You don't have to stick to Zinkl.

00:23:56.748 --> 00:24:00.888
You can use plain HTML. You can use another tool if you're very familiar with

00:24:01.458 --> 00:24:05.268
different tools. We just provide Zinkl because it's very easy out of the box.

00:24:05.568 --> 00:24:09.063
So .gitlab-ci is basically telling GitLab how to build your project

00:24:10.818 --> 00:24:11.238
and

00:24:12.528 --> 00:24:18.409
sort of, yeah. I'll show you. So it's sort of saying, "Okay, we're using Python

00:24:18.468 --> 00:24:23.238
because this program is built in Python, and then we're going to install the

00:24:23.328 --> 00:24:28.218
required plugins, and then we're going to build your project." So that's very easy.

00:24:28.233 --> 00:24:32.493
And then it's just saying everything in the public is we're now in the docs folder,

00:24:32.508 --> 00:24:35.238
and we need to build HTML into a public folder.

00:24:37.548 --> 00:24:39.977
All right. So let's talk

00:24:41.448 --> 00:24:44.508
about the configuration file and try some changes.

00:24:46.098 --> 00:24:50.118
The configuration is in a language called TOML, and it's just basically, it's got a

00:24:50.178 --> 00:24:51.168
key and value

00:24:52.428 --> 00:24:58.053
format. So the first word is always the sort of the setting key, the value you want

00:24:58.098 --> 00:25:02.298
to change, and then there's the value which goes in the quotes, which is what you

00:25:02.328 --> 00:25:08.298
want the setting to contain. So for example, site name is telling Zinkl, "Okay,

00:25:08.388 --> 00:25:11.418
my site name equals," and then whatever you want to call it.

00:25:11.508 --> 00:25:16.457
So you can put your name, you can put My Fabric Academy Journey. You can choose.

00:25:17.118 --> 00:25:22.428
This is basically deciding. And these values are sort of the ones I suggest you

00:25:22.488 --> 00:25:24.649
start with, and you can find them

00:25:26.149 --> 00:25:29.058
in the project. If you can't find the word, you just do a search.

00:25:29.208 --> 00:25:32.178
If you can't find it, then you can just write it on a new line somewhere.

00:25:33.768 --> 00:25:36.964
And basically, you can change your name, your description, and your author, and

00:25:36.978 --> 00:25:38.419
these apply to all the pages.

00:25:40.068 --> 00:25:44.538
The site URL, I would leave to whatever is written there, because this is basically

00:25:44.628 --> 00:25:48.858
the URL you have in the browser. It needs to know what your website URL is, so

00:25:49.548 --> 00:25:55.353
don't change that one. A key caveat is that these key settings are

00:25:55.399 --> 00:25:59.868
organized into sections. So if you look at site name, it's under the section called

00:25:59.928 --> 00:26:05.464
Project in square brackets. And then we have the color, the palette, which is

00:26:05.508 --> 00:26:07.173
choosing sort of the default color of

00:26:08.688 --> 00:26:08.988
text,

00:26:10.698 --> 00:26:16.143
is under project.theme. And then you have the selected font that you want for your

00:26:16.188 --> 00:26:18.678
whole website is under project.theme.font.

00:26:18.993 --> 00:26:23.868
And so these square brackets are important, and so you can't put site name under

00:26:23.899 --> 00:26:26.748
project.theme. It has to go under the correct.

00:26:28.488 --> 00:26:32.658
So for example, if I'm going to look at docs.

00:26:36.919 --> 00:26:42.738
If I go to their website, I just want to show you an example of-- So I'm going to

00:26:42.813 --> 00:26:43.743
their documentation,

00:26:45.468 --> 00:26:49.548
and I see, okay, fonts, and I'm reading, because this is what I would do.

00:26:49.698 --> 00:26:51.889
I would say, how do I do something? I want to change the font.

00:26:51.948 --> 00:26:55.698
And on their documentation, they're showing you, okay, so you need to change

00:26:55.728 --> 00:26:58.158
font.text to change the font under it.

00:26:58.218 --> 00:27:02.058
So the first thing I would do is in my configuration, I'm going to find this

00:27:02.598 --> 00:27:06.018
section in my configuration file if it exists. It might not.

00:27:06.889 --> 00:27:10.308
And so if I find this one, then I can put this text right under it.

00:27:11.658 --> 00:27:15.139
So this is showing you what you need to change, and you can match it with what you

00:27:15.198 --> 00:27:18.919
have. And so it's very important, this sort of structure of square brackets is the

00:27:19.248 --> 00:27:22.488
sections, and then sort of the setting, and then two quotes.

00:27:22.578 --> 00:27:24.363
Always close your quotes, open and close.

00:27:25.416 --> 00:27:30.246
And then you might, if you make a mistake, then your project will fail to build,

00:27:30.306 --> 00:27:31.986
and you'll get an error about it. And so

00:27:33.516 --> 00:27:37.266
just be, yeah, mindful of those changes of the TOML.

00:27:38.016 --> 00:27:42.696
I do link to a little validator that you can do if really you changed something, it

00:27:42.726 --> 00:27:45.576
didn't work. There's sort of a TOML checker here you can

00:27:48.096 --> 00:27:48.786
try out.

00:27:59.856 --> 00:28:00.786
I'm not sure what your--

00:28:02.076 --> 00:28:03.906
Dina, what was your question? Password?

00:28:07.506 --> 00:28:09.966
Your password for your accounts is in your email.

00:28:10.207 --> 00:28:11.046
If you

00:28:12.336 --> 00:28:15.156
can't find your email, you can always do a password reset.

00:28:16.746 --> 00:28:18.246
So basically, going back,

00:28:21.606 --> 00:28:23.677
we run all our accounts on FabLab Zero.

00:28:23.736 --> 00:28:29.016
So when you're trying to log in here, you can click the forgot password link and

00:28:29.076 --> 00:28:32.377
put in your email address, the one that you sent to coordination.

00:28:32.437 --> 00:28:37.356
So that's another way of getting access to your account is to just ask for a, reset

00:28:37.416 --> 00:28:40.177
your password. So I would suggest trying that.

00:28:41.556 --> 00:28:47.406
See if you get the email. Otherwise, yeah, I'll contact you.

00:28:53.286 --> 00:28:58.806
So, if you haven't, while I was talking, tried, you can go to the editor in GitLab,

00:28:58.927 --> 00:29:02.437
and I'm going to try now edit the TOML file.

00:29:03.216 --> 00:29:09.082
And so I can go to my TOML file if I hit site name, I'll put my name, and

00:29:09.156 --> 00:29:10.656
then I can put my...

00:29:13.746 --> 00:29:14.526
All right.

00:29:23.946 --> 00:29:25.627
I'm also going to change the copyright.

00:29:29.106 --> 00:29:30.546
Okay. Well,

00:29:32.046 --> 00:29:32.796
what do I do now?

00:29:35.556 --> 00:29:41.136
Because we're in the browser, we actually have to-- It's not automatically saving.

00:29:41.586 --> 00:29:45.516
We actually have to sort of intentionally commit and push our changes.

00:29:45.606 --> 00:29:49.416
So even though we're working in the browser in GitLab, we still have to tell

00:29:49.446 --> 00:29:52.536
GitLab, "Okay, I'm finished working on this file.

00:29:53.496 --> 00:29:59.226
I'm ready to sort of publish it to my website." And you'll see there's a little one

00:29:59.286 --> 00:30:03.336
here, which is basically telling me it's detected that one file changed.

00:30:04.326 --> 00:30:07.896
If I open this sort of sidebar, it's a sort of another view tab.

00:30:10.146 --> 00:30:14.226
It's listing here this one file is this field TOML.

00:30:14.766 --> 00:30:16.296
It's got an M, it means it's modified.

00:30:16.536 --> 00:30:20.106
If I click it, it's going to show me what changed.

00:30:20.166 --> 00:30:25.326
So you've got a minus and a plus. You've got two lines and showing it sort of the

00:30:25.356 --> 00:30:29.046
from to. So it's showing you what it was and what it became.

00:30:29.496 --> 00:30:30.366
And so it's like,

00:30:32.677 --> 00:30:36.486
I like to just go through, "Okay, yes, I did this." There was no everything looks

00:30:36.516 --> 00:30:40.161
good. You can compare the quotes match. It's another double checking.

00:30:40.685 --> 00:30:44.677
Especially when changing a TOML, you want to be really sure you didn't forget a

00:30:44.736 --> 00:30:46.880
closing tag. And comparing it to the previous

00:30:48.366 --> 00:30:52.446
line that changed, it's a good way to see if you missed anything.

00:30:55.656 --> 00:31:00.246
Then we have a commit message. This is sort of every save, every commit you do, you

00:31:00.306 --> 00:31:05.707
can put a sort of a comment to it, and this is just for you personally.

00:31:05.781 --> 00:31:10.386
It will be in the history, and it will be permanent, but it's a good way to know

00:31:11.646 --> 00:31:14.856
what was in these commits. So if you're going back and you have a long list of

00:31:15.216 --> 00:31:19.416
changes, you don't want to open each one to see, where did I change this file?

00:31:19.567 --> 00:31:22.866
So a good thing is to write a message that's good for you. So there's two ways.

00:31:23.736 --> 00:31:29.450
You can say, "I updated the TOML file," or you can say, "I changed my site name."

00:31:29.946 --> 00:31:33.846
You can be more descriptive, you can just be generic, or you can just say, "One,

00:31:33.877 --> 00:31:39.321
two, three." It's up to you. So you can say, "Updated a config file,"

00:31:40.506 --> 00:31:41.796
or you can say, "Updated,"

00:31:43.026 --> 00:31:47.196
or "Edited my name." You can write what you want.

00:31:47.210 --> 00:31:49.026
It's just personally for you, it makes no difference.

00:31:49.146 --> 00:31:52.086
And when we're ready with a message, you say commit and push.

00:31:52.836 --> 00:31:56.826
But very important, it has to say, "Push to main." Main is sort of,

00:31:58.207 --> 00:32:02.136
there can be lots of forks in this project. You can have lots of parallel work.

00:32:02.196 --> 00:32:05.766
You can do lots of things together at the same time, and so they call this

00:32:05.826 --> 00:32:11.796
branches, but generally 99% of the time, I think you should just work in main.

00:32:11.856 --> 00:32:16.056
So always be pushing to main because you only really want to work on one thing at a

00:32:16.116 --> 00:32:18.486
time and not have to deal with merging things together.

00:32:18.817 --> 00:32:20.707
So always make sure it's committing to main.

00:32:21.666 --> 00:32:26.796
If it doesn't say main, you can click the drop down and then select something else.

00:32:27.696 --> 00:32:33.306
Let me know if you don't see main. You can comment me now or raise your hand.

00:32:34.026 --> 00:32:39.156
So when you click commit and push, it's going to double ask you, are you sure?

00:32:39.187 --> 00:32:39.396
Because,

00:32:42.096 --> 00:32:43.866
but we're sure. We always want to connect.

00:32:43.986 --> 00:32:46.446
I think after this, the first time, it won't ask you again.

00:32:46.716 --> 00:32:49.146
So you're just like, "Yes, I want to commit to the main branch."

00:32:50.496 --> 00:32:51.666
And that should go through.

00:32:55.026 --> 00:32:57.306
And then, so you're thinking, okay,

00:32:59.437 --> 00:33:04.177
I'm now going to reload my commit list. I'm back on my project.

00:33:04.567 --> 00:33:07.957
Where's the refresh button? I'm going to refresh this page,

00:33:09.546 --> 00:33:13.716
and I'm going to see this is the new commit I made 19 seconds ago, update config,

00:33:14.496 --> 00:33:15.937
and I'm going to have the green check mark.

00:33:16.446 --> 00:33:19.356
Now let's dive into what this check mark is.

00:33:19.446 --> 00:33:24.111
It's related to every time you make a commit or you push from locally, we're going

00:33:24.127 --> 00:33:29.571
to actually build your project. And so, this is called a CI pipeline or

00:33:29.646 --> 00:33:33.396
pipelines, and it's basically for every commit you make, it's going to run that

00:33:33.486 --> 00:33:37.296
code that we had in that .CI YAML file in that if you saw in your project.

00:33:37.356 --> 00:33:41.226
So it's basically building your Zekel project, and you always want it to pass.

00:33:41.286 --> 00:33:45.636
So if it fails, then you sort of open it, you click-- it's going to have a red

00:33:45.786 --> 00:33:50.346
error message. And if you click it and go into it, you're going to see this deploy

00:33:50.466 --> 00:33:54.216
pages. It's called Pages. So we're going to click into it, and then you're just

00:33:54.245 --> 00:33:57.696
going to see a bunch of logs. This is sort of the server building your project,

00:33:57.756 --> 00:34:03.606
downloading the Zekel website and installing it, and then downloading your code.

00:34:03.666 --> 00:34:04.131
And then...

00:34:09.396 --> 00:34:11.317
So in my scenario, it's just successful.

00:34:11.346 --> 00:34:14.707
It's going to always be successful, but if it fails, then you will sort of at the

00:34:15.187 --> 00:34:21.036
bottom of this list of sort of the logs of the server building your project, it

00:34:21.067 --> 00:34:26.586
might have error. We couldn't find an image, or the TOML file had an error.

00:34:26.616 --> 00:34:30.187
And so sometimes these errors are a bit cryptic. I go back to that meme.

00:34:31.026 --> 00:34:32.406
It's like, what's this error mean?

00:34:32.466 --> 00:34:38.106
And so that might be a good-- That's good to read the error message, try and find

00:34:38.136 --> 00:34:41.586
the error message, and look at the commits. What did I actually change?

00:34:41.646 --> 00:34:43.417
Go back to see what you actually changed.

00:34:44.797 --> 00:34:47.346
Google it or ask AI, what's this error mean?

00:34:47.976 --> 00:34:50.601
And then not how do I fix it, but what does it mean?

00:34:50.677 --> 00:34:54.276
So then you can actually try and fix it yourself and sort of have that.

00:34:54.846 --> 00:34:58.866
So, keep in mind, it's always going to work, but if the pipelines you have to have

00:34:58.896 --> 00:35:02.646
in the back of your head, every commit is going to be building your project in a

00:35:02.706 --> 00:35:05.615
pipeline, on a server, and sometimes it may fail.

00:35:05.706 --> 00:35:10.416
So you'll always make sure that if your website doesn't seem updated, double-check.

00:35:10.476 --> 00:35:13.401
Did I get the green mark? Was it correct, or did it fail?

00:35:15.366 --> 00:35:17.346
Sometimes you get an email that it failed as well.

00:35:18.876 --> 00:35:24.381
Yeah. So, just for fun, I'll check up,

00:35:25.056 --> 00:35:28.086
and I should have my name. Yeah, I have my name now, so it worked.

00:35:31.656 --> 00:35:36.486
I think I'll also show you-- So, just double-check my slides where I am.

00:35:39.696 --> 00:35:41.226
Google Fonts. Yeah. So, I

00:35:43.236 --> 00:35:47.031
don't know where fonts are, so I can just do a Apple+F. I can do Find.

00:35:47.616 --> 00:35:52.866
And so I'm searching within this editor. I can do fonts, and there we go.

00:35:52.927 --> 00:35:56.976
Theme fonts here. So these are Google Fonts.

00:35:57.036 --> 00:35:59.797
You can go to google.com or

00:36:00.396 --> 00:36:04.056
fonts.google.com, I think.

00:36:05.556 --> 00:36:08.346
And basically, you can look through these fonts and find something you like, so you

00:36:08.406 --> 00:36:13.116
can sort of already have something suited to your style just by changing the font.

00:36:13.206 --> 00:36:16.101
